---
// Before you ask an AI about your health (2026-11-15). General cautions only; no diagnosis or treatment advice.
// Bases: the Data Protection Commission's guidance on AI and LLMs (18 July 2024), GDPR Article 9, HIQA's January 2026 draft (labelled as a draft)
// and the AI for Care strategy. No emergency phone number is given because none was read in a primary source.
import '../../../styles/ie-guide.css';
import IeLayout from '../../../layouts/IeLayout.astro';
import IeGuideFoot from '../../../components/IeGuideFoot.astro';
import IeFaq from '../../../components/IeFaq.astro';
import { guideSchema } from '../../../utils/ie-guide';
import { SRC } from '../../../data/ie/guides/milestones';
import { withBase } from '../../../utils/site';

const CHECKED = 'November 15, 2026';
const title = 'Asking an AI about your health in Ireland: six things to know';
const description =
  'An AI answer is not a diagnosis. Six things to know before you type health questions into a chatbot: urgency, privacy, where your data goes and your own records.';

const rights = [
  {
    h: '1. An AI answer is not a diagnosis',
    p: 'A chatbot cannot examine you or see your full history, and it can sound sure when it is wrong. The Data Protection Commission notes that AI products can produce inaccurate or biased information. Use a chatbot, at most, to prepare questions for your own doctor or nurse.',
  },
  {
    h: '2. Do not wait if it may be urgent',
    p: 'If you think you or someone else may be having a life-threatening emergency, call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait for a chatbot reply.',
  },
  {
    h: '3. What you type may leave your control',
    p: 'The Data Protection Commission’s guidance warns that, with AI tools, personal data typed in may be used in ways you did not expect, for example kept by the provider or used to train a model. Before you type health details into a service, read what it says about where the data goes, and ask whether you need to type them at all.',
  },
  {
    h: '4. Share less than you think you need to',
    p: 'Leave out your name, PPS number, date of birth, address and phone number, and photos that show your face or a document, unless you are sure the service is one you trust with them. Under the GDPR, data about your health is in a special category that gets extra protection (Article 9), but a service you use yourself may not be run by a hospital.',
  },
  {
    h: '5. You can ask a hospital how it uses AI',
    p: 'HIQA’s consultation draft and the Department of Health’s AI for Care strategy both stress transparency: telling people when, where and for what purpose AI is used in their care. If you want to know, ask. The draft says the guidance is also meant to help people using services understand what to expect.',
  },
  {
    h: '6. Bring your own records to your doctor',
    p: 'Keep your own reports, prescriptions and discharge summaries together. They help the doctor who treats you far more than a chatbot’s summary of them.',
  },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is it safe to paste my test results into a chatbot?',
    a: 'This page cannot say what a particular tool does with them. The Data Protection Commission’s guidance says organisations should understand what personal data an AI product uses, where it goes and whether it is kept or re-used. As an individual, read the privacy notice and remove your name and identifiers first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does a hospital have to tell me when AI is used in my care?',
    a: 'We did not find a rule in the documents we read that says every tool must be announced to every patient. HIQA’s draft and the AI for Care strategy stress transparency about when, where and why AI is used. We did not read the final HIQA guidance or Irish statutes. If you want to know, ask.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I rely on an AI app that says it is approved?',
    a: 'Ask exactly what is approved, by whom and for what use. A claim on an app store page is not the approval itself. HPRA says it monitors medical devices but does not approve them, so a device claim should come with conformity details.',
  },
];

const sources = [SRC.dpcAi, SRC.gdpr, SRC.hiqaDraft, SRC.aiForCare, SRC.hpraOverview];

const siteRootG = Astro.site ? Astro.site.href.replace(/\/$/, '') : 'https://hospital-ai-lab.com';
const schema = guideSchema({
  siteRoot: siteRootG,
  path: '/ie/patients/ai-health-consult/',
  headline: title,
  description,
  sources,
  published: '2026-11-15',
  modified: '2026-11-15',
  audience: 'Patients and their families',
  faq,
});
---

<IeLayout title={title} description={description} crumb="Asking AI about health" standalone updated={new Date('2026-11-15T00:00:00+00:00')} schema={schema}>
  <h1>Asking an AI about your health in Ireland: six things to know</h1>
  <p class="tg-lead">
    Many people now ask AI chatbots about symptoms, test results and medicines. This page does not tell you what to do about any health condition. It explains a few facts that help you use such tools more carefully.
  </p>
  <p class="tg-meta">Checked: {CHECKED} · For: patients and their families</p>

  <div class="tg-box tg-warn">
    This is general information. It is not legal advice and not medical advice for your situation. If you have a health concern, contact your own clinician or the health care organisation that treats you. In an emergency, call your local emergency number or go to the nearest emergency department.
  </div>

  <section class="tg-sec" aria-labelledby="h-rights">
    <h2 id="h-rights">Six things</h2>
    {
      rights.map((r) => (
        <>
          <h3>{r.h}</h3>
          <p>{r.p}</p>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <p class="tg-meta">What to expect when a hospital uses AI in your care: <a href={withBase('/ie/patients/ai-in-hospital/')}>what HIQA’s guidance and the EU rules say</a>.</p>

  <IeFaq items={faq} />

  <IeGuideFoot sources={sources} checked={CHECKED} patient />
</IeLayout>
